/**
 * Converts a String to a {@link Number} object.
 * This method attempts to parse the input string into the most appropriate
 * {@link Number} subclass ({@link Integer}, {@link Long}, {@link Float},
 * {@link Double}, {@link BigInteger}, or {@link BigDecimal}) based on its
 * format and content.
 *
 * <p>The parsing logic handles various number formats including:
 * <ul>
 *     <li>Decimal integers (e.g., "123", "-45")</li>
 *     <li>Hexadecimal integers (e.g., "0xAF", "-0x10")</li>
 *     <li>Floating-point numbers (e.g., "1.23", "-4.5e-2")</li>
 *     <li>Numbers with type suffixes: 'l'/'L' for {@link Long} (or {@link BigInteger} if too large),
 *         'f'/'F' for {@link Float}, 'd'/'D' for {@link Double}.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>If no type suffix is provided, the method attempts to parse the number
 * progressively:
 * <ul>
 *     <li>For integer-like strings (no decimal or exponent), it tries {@link Integer}, then {@link Long}, then {@link BigInteger}.</li>
 *     <li>For floating-point-like strings (with decimal or exponent), it tries {@link Float}, then {@link Double}, then {@link BigDecimal}.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>Precision is considered for floating-point numbers. If a {@link Float} or
 * {@link Double} cannot represent the number from the string exactly (i.e.,
 * it results in infinity, zero for a non-zero original value, or a different
 * value when converted back to a {@link BigDecimal}), a more precise type
 * ({@link Double} or {@link BigDecimal}) will be attempted.
